Looking at the type declarations and notes, I need to:

1. Find all groups on the manager that have a track animating the given property of the given target.
2. Remove those specific tracks from each group's `_propertyMixer` so the property is no longer written, while leaving all other tracks (other properties, other targets) intact.

The key insight from note 47 is that in a property group, `targetedAnimations` entries store the *leaf* target (the object owning the last path segment) and the last segment as the path. The `_propertyMixer` tracks use `mixTarget()` (returns that leaf object) and `mixProperty` (the last segment) for the same purpose. So I resolve the user's dotted path to its leaf target and leaf property, then filter matching tracks out of each group's mixer.

FILE: app/src/release.ts
```ts
import { getAnimationGroups } from "@babylonjs/lite";
import type { AnimationManager } from "@babylonjs/lite";

export function releaseProperty(manager: AnimationManager, target: object, path: string): void {
    const parts = path.split(".");
    const leafProperty = parts[parts.length - 1];

    // Resolve the object that owns the last path segment.
    let leafTarget: object = target;
    for (let i = 0; i < parts.length - 1; i++) {
        leafTarget = (leafTarget as Record<string, unknown>)[parts[i]] as object;
    }

    const groups = getAnimationGroups(manager);

    for (const group of groups) {
        const mixer = group._propertyMixer;
        if (!mixer) continue;

        const tracks = mixer[0];
        const filtered = tracks.filter(
            (track) => !(track.mixTarget() === leafTarget && track.mixProperty === leafProperty),
        );

        if (filtered.length !== tracks.length) {
            group._propertyMixer = [filtered, mixer[1], mixer[2], mixer[3], mixer[4]];
        }
    }
}
```

I wrote `src/release.ts` which resolves the dotted path to its leaf target and leaf property, then iterates every group on the manager and filters out any `_propertyMixer` tracks whose `mixTarget()` and `mixProperty` match, replacing the group's mixer with a new tuple that excludes them. I was not sure whether the `_propertyMixer` tuple's trailing numeric fields (indices 1–4) encode anything that depends on the track count (e.g., a pre-computed stride sum), but given they appear to be static parameters set at group creation time and the tracks array is what gets iterated at update time, I'm confident the replacement is safe.

